para('The educational case for repetition',r'''The educational case for repetition is contingent. \citet{allen} find less negative estimated achievement effects in stronger research designs; \citet{goos} report an average effect near zero alongside heterogeneity across contexts, comparisons and outcomes. Academic difficulties therefore do not uniquely determine whether repetition is an appropriate response. Our framework distinguishes teachers' assessment of difficulties if the student progresses from their assessment of the gains and costs of making the student repeat. Studies of recommendations and studies of the consequences of actual retention address complementary questions.''')
a=s.index('The first literature concerns');b=s.index('\n\n',a);s=s[:a]+s[b+2:]
para('We study 2,705 teachers',r'''We study 2,604 teachers evaluating profiles with six attributes. Four questions organize the contribution: which attributes change recommendations within teachers; how academic and behavioral signals combine; where teachers disagree on comparable cases; and how measured teacher information and observed response patterns contribute to decision variation. The first two questions form the within-teacher results, the third motivates the between-teacher analysis, and the fourth connects teacher characteristics to the decomposition. Harshness, defined as the average departure from other teachers' recommendations on the same six-attribute profiles, links these analyses.''')
para('The results distinguish all three levels.',r'''The results distinguish common criteria from their application. Failed subjects and low competences have the largest within-teacher coefficients, 54.7 and 27.1 percentage points, and reinforce one another. Attendance and discipline also matter (6.7 and 4.8 points). Complex social background moderates repetition recommendations ($-1.8$ points), whereas the average gender coefficient is small and statistically indistinguishable from zero. The contrast with higher repetition incidence among boys and disadvantaged students in population data \citep{oecd} is compatible with disparities arising partly before the final promotion judgment, rather than from an additional penalty attached directly to these characteristics in otherwise comparable cases.''')
para('Secondly, harshness varies',r'''Harshness varies substantially across teachers, and disagreement is particularly pronounced for several failed subjects combined with adequate competences. Demographic and professional information and reported beliefs explain less than five percent of observed harshness variation. The decision-level decomposition assigns the largest share to student information, with substantial additional components associated with overall harshness and different responses to attributes. The contribution is to connect common criteria, disagreement on comparable cases and differences in how those criteria are applied.''')
para('The survey was collected',r'''The survey was collected between 14 March and 29 April 2025 among primary and secondary teachers in one Spanish region. The study plan specifies recruitment through the regional education authority and schools forwarding an online questionnaire. Participation was voluntary. After setting aside 905 entries with minimal initial engagement, 4,037 records form the participation-flow denominator; 3,103 contain at least one card response, including examples. The favorable-example screening rule excludes 379 records. A further 120 lack a recoverable experimental response, leaving 2,604 teacher records with 20,467 recommendations. Appendix~\ref{app:sample} documents this reconstruction, participation and comparisons with regional teacher statistics.''')
add_before('The final questionnaire asks',r'''Teachers were assigned to one of eight predefined sets of eight cards, with randomized presentation order within the set. The 64 set positions represent 56 distinct six-attribute profiles: eight profiles occur in two sets, and eight of the 64 possible combinations are absent. Gender and social background are balanced within each set, but the academic and behavioral composition differs across sets (Appendix~\ref{app:sample}). The survey also assigned policy-information contexts, including a control condition. This article pools recommendations across those contexts; a control-only attribute comparison is reported in the appendix.''')
add_before('The final questionnaire asks',r'''The student-profile decision task was documented in the study's preregistration \citep{registry}. We use this registered task to study how student information and differences between teachers structure recommendations. The reconstruction and the covariance comparisons reported here were developed for the present analysis.''')
para('Before the task, teachers are shown',r'''Before the task, teachers are shown a favorable example and an example combining adverse academic and behavioral characteristics. We exclude records recommending repetition for the favorable example assigned to that respondent. We do not exclude teachers who recommend progression for the adverse example, since they may oppose repetition even in difficult cases. The rule is an outcome-based analytical screen, not independent evidence of inattention; the appendix therefore also reports results without it.''')
add_before('\\section{Decision framework',r'''We reconstruct experimental responses from each record's eight-position presentation sequence, keeping only assigned card fields. When an assigned card and that respondent's example share a stored response field, reconstruction B retains that value once, assuming that the recommendation is unchanged between the example and experimental presentation. There are 656 such retained observations. Excluding these shared values provides a sensitivity check; it removes ambiguity but also selectively removes extreme profiles. The main sample retains up to eight observed recommendations per teacher: 2,520 teachers have eight and 84 have one to seven.''')
para('We estimate linear probability models',r'''We estimate linear probability models with teacher fixed effects and standard errors clustered by teacher. The fixed effects absorb differences in average recommendation levels, including any constant set or policy-context effect within teacher. Attribute coefficients summarize conditional contrasts across the implemented profiles, rather than averages over independently randomized attributes in the full factorial. Teachers with only one usable response contribute to descriptive harshness but not to these coefficients; the main specification contains 20,452 decisions from 2,589 teachers. The fixed effects do not require attribute responses to be identical across policy contexts.''')
# Profile references: all identical six-dimensional profiles pooled, original ID retained for audit.
s=s.replace('same recorded card:', 'same six-attribute profile, pooling identifiers with identical attributes:')
s=s.replace('same-card reference','same-profile reference').replace('same-card references','same-profile references')
para('Harshness summarizes the direction',r'''Harshness summarizes the direction of a teacher's departures from peers. Same-profile disagreement asks whether two distinct teachers evaluating an identical six-attribute profile recommend different outcomes. We calculate this probability within each profile and average it by response frequency, including within four categories formed by failed subjects and competences. Thus repeated profiles under different set identifiers contribute to the same reference. The appendix checks the alternative of retaining identifier-specific references.''')
para('All three specifications use',r'''All three specifications use the same 2,164 complete cases. We use HC3 robust standard errors to allow for differences in residual variability across teachers, with an additional adjustment for unusual combinations of teacher characteristics.''')
s=s.replace('18,608 decisions of the 2,157','17,312 decisions of the 2,164')
para('where $f(x_i)$ contains',r'''where $f(x_i)$ contains the six student attributes and all 15 pairwise interactions, $z_j$ contains professional and demographic characteristics, and $a_j$ contains reported beliefs. The six elements of $b_j$ are teacher-specific deviations from the average attribute coefficients. The primary model estimates a separate variance for each deviation and sets their covariances to zero, so $\Sigma$ is diagonal. Predictors $\widetilde{x}_i$ are centered at their means across the complete-case decisions. For example, teachers with the same overall harshness can attach different additional weight to absenteeism: its common coefficient captures the average response, while its random slope allows that response to differ across teachers. All six attributes receive random slopes. The model includes neither an additional teacher random intercept nor experimental-assignment indicators.''')
add_before('For the allocation, let',r'''We compare three prespecified-for-this-revision covariance structures on the same data: six independent slopes; correlated slopes within academic, behavioral and sociodemographic pairs; and an unrestricted covariance matrix. The latter two converge to boundary solutions. We therefore use the stable interior diagonal specification as primary and report all three allocations in Appendix~\ref{app:prediction}. Zero covariances are a simplifying working assumption, not a conclusion that teachers' responses to different attributes are unrelated.''')
s=s.replace('The second term measures the joint contribution of attribute sensitivities over the observed profiles, including their covariances.', 'The second term measures the joint contribution of attribute sensitivities over the observed profiles; in the diagonal specification it is the sum of each slope variance multiplied by its centered predictor\'s mean square.')
para('Attendance and conduct retain',r'''Attendance and conduct retain a role beyond the academic indicators. Frequent unjustified absence adds 6.72 points, and serious disciplinary problems add 4.78 points. Their smaller magnitudes do not make them incidental controls: teachers incorporate information about engagement and behavior when deciding between promotion and repetition. The coefficients alone do not distinguish expected educational gains, perceived readiness or normative considerations about conduct.''')
s=s.replace('only 0.26 percentage points','only 0.40 percentage points').replace('by 1.88 points','by 1.76 points')
para('The interaction specification in',r'''The interaction specification in Table~\ref{tab:interactions} shows that failed subjects and low competences reinforce one another: their interaction adds 11.07 percentage points and remains significant after Holm adjustment across the three interaction tests. The interaction of failures with discipline is smaller (2.18 points) but also survives this adjustment; the attendance interaction is imprecise. Thus combining academic difficulties produces the strongest reinforcement, alongside a more modest additional role for discipline when subjects are failed. The next section examines cases in which academic signals point in different directions.
\inputtab{revised}{interactions}{Combining academic and behavioral information within teachers}{tab:interactions}''')
para('Figure~\\ref{fig:harshness} introduces',r'''Figure~\ref{fig:harshness} introduces the distribution of harshness. Teachers are compared with peers evaluating the same six-attribute profiles. The unweighted teacher mean is close to zero ($-0.12$ percentage points), with a standard deviation of 19.00 points. The lower tail identifies teachers who recommend repetition less often than peers for the profiles they answered; it does not, by itself, establish general opposition to repetition.
\plot{revised}{harshness_distribution}{Distribution of harshness among 2,604 teachers, using 12.5-percentage-point bins centered on zero and equal weight per teacher. Negative values indicate fewer repetition recommendations than peers evaluating the same profiles; positive values indicate more.}{fig:harshness}''')
para('Same-profile disagreement shows',r'''Same-profile disagreement shows where judgments diverge. Across the 56 profiles, its frequency-weighted value is 25.22 percent. Table~\ref{tab:academic} locates the principal boundary: for several failed subjects combined with adequate competences, two teachers disagree on the same profile in 49.12 percent of pairs. When neither academic difficulty is present, disagreement is 5.60 percent. Recommendation rates also differ asymmetrically across the conflicting categories: 51.52 percent for several failures with adequate competences, compared with 23.79 percent for low competences with few failures. Large average responses to academic information therefore coexist with disagreement when the signals conflict.
\inputtab{revised}{academic}{Academic profiles and disagreement on the same six-attribute profile}{tab:academic}''')
para('Tables~\\ref{tab:tercsummary}',r'''Tables~\ref{tab:tercsummary} and \ref{tab:tercslopes} describe differences across the harshness distribution. Teachers in the lower and upper terciles have mean harshness of $-20.93$ and 19.36 points. Their demographic and professional profiles overlap substantially: mean age differs by about two years, and women constitute approximately two-thirds of each group. Secondary teachers account for 42.9 percent of the lower tercile and 53.7 percent of the upper. Agreement that repeaters receive too many resources rises from 3.06 to 4.02 on the 0--10 scale. Effort attribution averages close to seven in all three groups.
\inputtab{revised}{tercile_summary}{Teacher characteristics across observed harshness terciles}{tab:tercsummary}''')
para('The attribute coefficients differ',r'''Attribute coefficients differ across these descriptive groups. The failed-subject coefficient is about 32 points in the lower tercile and 65--67 in the other two; the competence coefficient stays between 26 and 29 points. Absence has a positive coefficient in all three terciles, largest in the middle group. Discipline has positive coefficients in the lower and middle groups but a negative coefficient in the upper group. Because terciles are formed from the same responses, these regressions describe observed patterns rather than identifying three distinct teacher types. The continuous analysis below asks whether observed teacher information helps explain the overall differences.
\inputtab{revised}{tercile_slopes}{Within-teacher attribute coefficients across harshness terciles}{tab:tercslopes}''')
